---
title: Images
summary: '@ogygia/image encodes your images at build: AVIF and WebP over a width ladder, a tiny blur placeholder, and a srcset worked out from how the image sits on the page. One component renders them as server HTML, with no client script. Markdown images and frontmatter covers work the same way.'
related:
  - content/collections
  - content/markdown
  - reference/api
---

`@ogygia/image` turns image files into responsive images at build time. You say how an image sits on the page. The package writes the files, the `srcset`, the `sizes`, the width and height, the placeholder and the loading hints.

- **Encoded at build.** Each image is written as AVIF and WebP at the widths the page can use. The files are hashed and shipped like any other asset.
- **Layout intent, not numbers.** You pick `constrained`, `fullWidth` or `fixed`, and at most a width. You never write a `srcset`.
- **No layout shift.** Every image carries its width and height, so the page keeps its space.
- **A blur placeholder** of about 100 bytes paints until the image arrives.
- **No client JavaScript.** `<Img>` renders plain HTML on the server: a `<picture>` with its `<source>`s. It writes no inline event handler, so it passes a strict CSP.
- **Markdown too.** With `@ogygia/content`, `![alt](./photo.jpg)` in a post and `cover: ./cover.jpg` in its frontmatter become the same responsive images.

## Install

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i @ogygia/image
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add @ogygia/image
```

:::

List the plugin beside `ogygia()`, before `sveltekit()`:

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { content } from '@ogygia/content/vite';
import { image } from '@ogygia/image/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [ogygia(), content({ markdown: {} }), image({ markdown: { width: 720 } }), sveltekit()]
});
```

`content()` is only needed for markdown and frontmatter images. Add the macro's types next to ogygia's:

```ts title=src/ogygia.d.ts
/// <reference types="ogygia/types" />
/// <reference types="@ogygia/image/types" />
```

The encoder is [sharp](https://sharp.pixelplumbing.com), installed with the package.

## An image in a component

`import.meta.og.image(path)` reads an image file at build and gives you its descriptor. The path is relative to the file that calls it, like an import. Pass the descriptor to `<Img>`:

```svelte title=src/routes/+page.svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { Img } from '@ogygia/image';

  const hero = import.meta.og.image('../lib/images/hero.jpg');
  const avatar = import.meta.og.image('../lib/images/avatar.jpg', { width: 48 });
</script>

<Img of={hero} layout="fullWidth" priority alt="The harbor at dusk" />
<Img of={avatar} layout="fixed" width={48} alt="" />
```

The arguments must be written out in place: a string path and an object of plain values. The build reads them before any code runs, so a variable or a `${…}` in the path is a build error (`image_macro_not_literal`). A path that does not exist fails the build too (`image_not_found`). Call the macro in the component's `<script>`, not in its markup.

For many images at once, `import.meta.og.image.glob(pattern)` returns every match, keyed by path:

```ts
const gallery = import.meta.og.image.glob('./gallery/*.jpg');
```

A pattern that matches nothing fails the build. To pick an image at run time, make every candidate with a glob and choose among them.

## `<Img>`

```svelte
<Img of={hero} alt="The harbor at dusk" />
```

| Prop          | Default         | What it does                                                                                  |
| ------------- | --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `of`          | required        | The descriptor: from the macro, a markdown image, or a frontmatter image.                     |
| `alt`         | required        | What the image shows. Use `""` for a decorative image.                                        |
| `layout`      | `'constrained'` | How the image sits on the page (below).                                                       |
| `width`       | none            | Display width in CSS pixels.                                                                  |
| `height`      | none            | Display height in CSS pixels. With `width`, the image fills that box and is cropped to it.    |
| `priority`    | `false`         | The page's main image (its LCP). It loads eagerly, at high priority, preloaded from the head. |
| `placeholder` | `true`          | Paint the blur placeholder until the image arrives.                                           |
| `sizes`       | derived         | Replaces the `sizes` the layout works out, for a layout the three intents cannot say.         |

`class`, `id`, `style`, `title`, `crossorigin`, `referrerpolicy` and `draggable` pass through to the `<img>`. Other attributes do not: a spread onto an `<img>` makes Svelte write inline `onload`/`onerror` handlers, which a strict CSP blocks.

### Layouts

- **`constrained`** (the default): as wide as `width`, or as the image itself when you leave `width` out. It shrinks with its container and never grows past that width.
- **`fullWidth`**: the container's full width, on every screen. Use it for heroes and banners.
- **`fixed`**: exactly `width` × `height` CSS pixels, at 1× and 2× density. Use it for avatars and icons.

The `srcset` and `sizes` follow from the layout. A `constrained` image 400 pixels wide asks for about 400 pixels on a 1× screen and 800 on a 2× one, never the 2048-pixel file. With both `width` and `height`, the image is cropped to that box (`object-fit: cover`), and the files it picks cover the box's width.

### `priority`

Give `priority` to the one image that is the page's largest paint, usually the hero. It turns off lazy loading, sets `fetchpriority="high"` and adds a `<link rel="preload">` for the right file to the head. Every other image loads lazily and decodes off the main thread. The profiler's page insights flag a largest paint that is still lazy (`lcp-lazy`).

### What it renders

```html
<picture>
  <source type="image/avif" srcset="/_app/immutable/assets/hero-640.Bx1.avif 640w, …" sizes="100vw" />
  <img src="/_app/immutable/assets/hero-1280.Cq2.webp" srcset="…webp 640w, …" sizes="100vw"
       width="2400" height="1350" alt="The harbor at dusk" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
       data-og-img="fullWidth" style="background-image:url(data:image/webp;base64,…)" />
</picture>
```

The layout's own CSS is a zero-specificity rule on `data-og-img`, so any class of yours wins over it.

## Formats and options

By default each image is written as AVIF (quality 60) and WebP (quality 80), and the `<img>` fallback is WebP. Some files are special:

- **An animated GIF or WebP** is written as animated WebP only.
- **An SVG** is passed through as it is: it already scales.
- **A transparent image** gets no blur placeholder, since the blur would show through.

The plugin's options are every image's defaults. One image's own options, in the macro's second argument, win over them:

| Option        | Where         | Default                                  | What it does                                                                                       |
| ------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `quality`     | plugin, image | 60 for AVIF, 80 for the rest             | Encoder quality, 1 to 100.                                                                         |
| `formats`     | plugin, image | `['avif', 'webp']`                       | The formats to write, best first: `'avif'`, `'webp'`, `'png'`, `'jpeg'`.                           |
| `placeholder` | plugin, image | `true`                                   | Make the blur placeholder.                                                                         |
| `breakpoints` | plugin        | `[320, 480, 640, 960, 1280, 1600, 2048]` | The width ladder a responsive image is written at, capped at the image's own width.                |
| `markdown`    | plugin        | `{}`                                     | Markdown images: `{ width }` is the prose column's width in CSS pixels; `false` turns them off.    |
| `width`       | image         | none                                     | The widest this image is ever shown. Only widths up to twice it are written, not the whole ladder. |
| `ops`         | image         | none                                     | Pixel operations, applied before resizing (below).                                                 |

An unknown option or a wrong value fails the build with the key it did not expect (`image_option_unknown`, `image_option_invalid`), so a typo never goes unnoticed.

`ops` takes sharp's operations of the same names: `aspect` (crop to a ratio such as `16 / 9` or `'16/9'`) with `position` (`'center'`, `'top'`, `'bottom'`, `'left'`, `'right'`, or the smart crops `'attention'` and `'entropy'`), `rotate` (a multiple of 90), `flip`, `flop`, `grayscale`, `blur`, `sharpen`, `normalize`, `negate`, `gamma`, `tint` and `modulate`.

```ts
const card = import.meta.og.image('./team.jpg', {
  quality: 70,
  ops: { aspect: 4 / 3, position: 'attention', grayscale: true }
});
```

## Markdown images

With `content({ markdown })` and `image()` both listed, a local image in a markdown file renders through `<Img>`, with no import and no component in the post:

```md title=src/content/blog/my-post/+post.md
---
title: My post
cover: ./cover.jpg
---

![The harbor in the evening](./harbor.jpg "Seen from the pier")
```

- **Paths are relative to the post**, like an import. `../other-post/diagram.png` reaches into another folder. A name with spaces or accents works, written as is or URL-encoded (`./my%20photo.jpg`).
- **A missing file fails the build** (`image_not_found`), naming the post and the path.
- **`markdown.width`** is your prose column's width. Every markdown image is laid out as `constrained` at that width, so a phone and a laptop each get a file that fits. Leave it out and each image is as wide as itself.
- **Remote and site-absolute images** (`https://…`, `/static/x.png`) stay plain `<img>` tags, and the `img` slot of `site({ components })` still gets them.
- **HTML `<img>` tags in markdown** that name a local file fail the build (`image_markdown_html`): they would ship as a broken link. Write them as `![alt](./photo.jpg)`.
- **`import.meta.og.md(text)`** renders markdown from a string, which has no file to resolve against, so a local image there fails the build (`image_markdown_no_file`). Use a site-absolute URL there, or move the markdown into a `.md` file.

A `.md` post with a local image renders a component, so it is compiled as a Svelte component rather than kept as static HTML: with `content({ markdown: { region: true } })`, such a post leaves the static region path. A post without a local image still takes it.

To turn markdown images off, pass `image({ markdown: false })`. Markdown images stay plain `<img>` tags then, and every post can take the static path again.

## Frontmatter images

A frontmatter value that starts with `./` or `../` and ends in an image extension becomes a descriptor at build, wherever it sits (a nested object or a list works too). Other strings stay strings: `images/share.png` or `diagram.png` are left alone. A missing file fails the build.

In the collection's schema, accept the descriptor with `isImage`:

```ts title=src/lib/server/blog.ts
import { content, markdown } from '@ogygia/content';
import { isImage, type ImageDescriptor } from '@ogygia/image';
import * as v from 'valibot';

export const blog = content({
  loader: markdown(import.meta.glob('../../content/blog/*/+post.md', { eager: true }), {
    id: (key) => key.split('/').at(-2) ?? key
  }),
  schema: v.object({
    title: v.string(),
    cover: v.custom<ImageDescriptor>(isImage)
  })
});
```

Then render it like any other image:

```svelte title=src/routes/blog/[slug]/+page.svelte
<Img of={data.cover} layout="fullWidth" priority alt={data.title} />
```

## Outside `<Img>`

`layOut(descriptor, { layout, width, height, sizes })` returns what `<Img>` renders: `src`, `srcset`, `sizes`, `width`, `height`, the `<source>` list and the preload. Use it for a CSS background, an Open Graph tag or a component of your own:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { layOut } from '@ogygia/image';
  const photo = import.meta.og.image('./photo.jpg', { width: 1200 });
  const laid = layOut(photo, { layout: 'fullWidth' });
</script>

<section style="background-image: url({laid.src})">…</section>
```

## Dev and build

In dev nothing is encoded up front. Each variant's URL points at `/@og-image/…`, and the dev server encodes it the first time the browser asks, then keeps it in the build cache (`node_modules/.ogygia`). An edit to the image file gives it a new URL.

At build every variant is encoded once, cached by the file's content and the options, and imported as an asset, so Vite names, hashes and ships it under `_app/immutable/assets`. A second build reuses the cache.

## Moving from Astro's `astro:assets`

| Astro                                               | ogygia                                                          |
| --------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `import hero from './hero.jpg'`                     | `const hero = import.meta.og.image('./hero.jpg')`               |
| `<Image src={hero} alt="…" />`                      | `<Img of={hero} alt="…" />`                                     |
| `<Picture formats={['avif', 'webp']} />`            | `<Img>`: AVIF and WebP are the default                          |
| `widths={[600, 1200]}`, `sizes="…"`                 | `layout` and `width`; `sizes` only as an escape hatch           |
| `layout="full-width"` / `"constrained"` / `"fixed"` | `layout="fullWidth"` / `"constrained"` / `"fixed"`              |
| `loading="eager"` + `fetchpriority="high"`          | `priority`                                                      |
| `getImage({ src })`                                 | `layOut(descriptor, { … })`                                     |
| `image()` in a content schema                       | `v.custom<ImageDescriptor>(isImage)`, with a `./` or `../` path |
| `import.meta.glob('./*.jpg', { eager: true })`      | `import.meta.og.image.glob('./*.jpg')`                          |
| `![alt](./photo.jpg)` in `.md` / `.mdx`             | the same, with `content({ markdown })` and `image()` listed     |

A few differences to know when you move a site:

- **Frontmatter paths need `./` or `../`.** Astro's `image()` reads any path; here a bare `cover.png` stays a string. Astro posts usually write `./cover.png` already.
- **Animated GIFs** become animated WebP, never AVIF. There is no option to turn them off.
- **Remote images** are not supported yet: a URL in the macro fails the build (`image_remote_unsupported`). Markdown leaves remote images as plain `<img>`.
- **`<Img>` takes a fixed set of attributes** (listed above). A `data-*` or `aria-*` attribute is not passed through.
- **MDX components** are not part of `@ogygia/content`'s markdown. Rename `.mdx` posts to `.md` (or `.svx`, which takes Svelte components).
